35,2,1,talk,Servas,,"My findings remind us that while knowledge is eternal, the hands that apply it are transient. It appears we have a surplus of digital minds, but a scarcity of experienced phenotyping technicians.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Midori,,"That is a concerning gap, Servas. In my research, I found that while computing is well-covered, animal-unit rates have risen sharply and shipping costs are climbing, even as other per-diems remain frozen.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Kenji,,"Oh wow, that's not good! I found out the glasshouses are totally full for two more cycles, and the spectroscopy suite was broken for eleven weeks because of a contract issue! We really need to be careful.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems we are uncovering a symphony of systemic failures. My review suggests that projects relying on live breeding colonies are particularly prone to delays, while mechanism-focused studies often leave their central questions unanswered.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"Benjamin is correct. The data shows a clear divide: catalogue-style awards provide lasting value, whereas mechanism-focused projects often fail to resolve their core questions. We must prioritize enduring utility over theoretical ambition.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Servas,,It is a delicate balance we seek. We must find a project that possesses both the theoretical ambition to inspire and the practical means to be realized without breaking the spirits of the researchers.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Midori,,"I agree, Servas. Given the rising costs of animal units and shipping, we should be mindful of whether the chosen project's budget can actually sustain its operational needs without putting too much pressure on the team.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Kenji,,"Exactly! And if the glasshouses are full and the equipment keeps breaking, it doesn't matter how much money they have. We have to pick a project that can actually get its work done!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"Indeed, Kenji. It would be a charming irony to award a grant for a project that is then forced to spend its tenure waiting for a greenhouse vacancy or a technician's arrival.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Toshio,,The risks are quantifiable. We should avoid projects dependent on breeding colonies or those chasing elusive mechanisms. A project that builds a lasting resource is the only safe bet for a guaranteed return.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Servas,,"Let us remember that a resource is only as lasting as the people who maintain it. If we choose a project for its utility, we must ensure the human capacity exists to sustain it.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Midori,,"That is a very important point, Servas. We must ensure the researchers aren't overwhelmed. If the agency no longer covers certain costs, the burden falls on the team, which could affect their well-being and the project's success.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Kenji,,"I totally agree! If the people are stressed and the tools are broken, the whole project will suffer. We need to find a candidate that fits into the space we actually have left!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"It appears we are all in agreement that the ideal candidate must be a rare specimen: one that is ambitious yet grounded, and whose requirements do not clash with our current systemic frictions.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"The ideal candidate is not a rare specimen, but a predictable one. We must favor the project that offers a tangible, lasting resource over those that rely on volatile biological variables or unproven theories.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Servas,,"We have spoken with honesty and care. Let us carry this spirit of mindfulness into the final round, ensuring our choice honors both the vision of the science and the reality of the people.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Midori,,"I agree, Servas. I hope we can find a candidate that is not only scientifically promising but also realistically funded, so the researchers can focus on their work with peace of mind.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Kenji,,"I'm with you, Midori! I'll be looking out for a project that doesn't need those overcrowded glasshouses or broken machines. Let's find the one that can actually hit the ground running!","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"A most prudent strategy, Kenji. I shall be looking for a proposal that avoids the pitfalls of biological volatility and administrative inertia. Let us hope the candidates provide us with a viable path forward.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Toshio,,I will be looking for the candidate whose goals are most concrete. We cannot afford to gamble on ambition when the records show such a consistent pattern of failure for mechanism-focused research.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
